WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.960
America has celebrated Memorial Day for
at least 150 years.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.730
It's a post-Civil War holiday that used
to be called "Decoration Day". The name

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:17.540
morphed over time as the holiday grew
post-World War I to commemorate all

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:22.430
who have died in service to the country.
Remembering our fallen soldiers is more

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:26.540
than nostalgia: It's about re-engaging
with the principles that define America.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.860
Because Memorial Day renews the memory
of those who gave their lives that this

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.980
nation might live,
it reminds us that our nation is

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:39.170
dedicated to liberty and equality. Our
national cemeteries at Arlington and

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:43.039
elsewhere make it a point to specify the
individual names of fallen soldiers on

00:00:43.039 --> 00:00:46.879
those rows on rows of headstones, to
recognize the equal worthiness of each

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:52.100
of its dead soldiers to be remembered by
name. Not all democracies have done this:

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:56.420
In ancient Athens, dead soldiers were
nameless, because it was only the city of

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.620
Athens that was meant to be remembered.
It matters how a government thinks of

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:06.040
and treats its military dead, because it
reveals how it thinks about citizens and government.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.340
Do you think Americans have
lost sight of the meaning of Memorial

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:13.850
Day? Let us know in your comments. Also,
let us know what other topics you'd like

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:18.050
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and
be sure to like and subscribe for more

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:21.550
research and videos from AEI.

